%[text] # Offline Reinforcement Learning for Clinical Treatment Decisions
%[text] This example shows how to train, penalize, and audit a tabular offline reinforcement learning policy for a sepsis treatment workflow. The demo uses **synthetic sepsis treatment records**. If `sepsis_training_data.csv` exists, the live script loads it as a cache. If the CSV file is missing, the live script generates synthetic data automatically and saves a new cache.
%[text] Sepsis, sometimes called blood poisoning, is a potentially life-threatening condition in which the body's dysregulated response to infection injures its own tissues and organs. Because sepsis can evolve quickly, a clinical AI assistant could help surface evidence-supported treatment options for timely review while making uncertainty and data support visible to the clinician. For clinical background, see the [Sepsis-3 consensus definitions](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.0287).
%[text] The target user is a clinician, a clinical AI evaluator, or a MATLAB user who wants to understand the safety workflow around offline reinforcement learning. 
%[text] By the end of the example, you can identify how historical treatment records become reinforcement learning experiences, how a Q-table is learned from offline data, how a count-based conservative penalty can limit unsupported recommendations, and how the evidence counts and the ensemble disagreement help you decide when to trust a recommendation.
%[text] #### Reinforcement learning terms
%[text] Reinforcement learning terms map naturally to clinical review terms in this example (here, both the action and the state belong to finite sets):
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[209,-1]}
%[text] | **Reinforcement learning term** | **Clinical interpretation** |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | **State** | A simplified snapshot of the patient condition, such as early sepsis, refractory shock, or floor-ready recovery |
%[text] | **Action** | A treatment choice recorded at that state, such as fluids, antibiotics, vasopressors, or discharge |
%[text] | **Reward** | A numeric outcome signal that favors recovery and penalizes deterioration or death |
%[text] | **Policy** | A treatment rule that maps each clinical state to a recommended action |
%[text] | **Offline data** | Retrospective electronic medical record (EMR)-style records used for learning without experimenting on live patients |
%[text] | **Safety envelope** | The part of the state-action space with enough historical support to consider a recommendation |
%[text:table]
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Contents"}
%%
%[text] ## Prepare Synthetic Clinical Data
%[text] In this example, you use **synthetic data** so that no real patient records appear. If `sepsis_training_data.csv` exists, the example loads this cached data. Otherwise, the example generates the data and saves it for later use.
%[text] The EMR-style table contains one row per recorded treatment decision. Each row includes a patient identifier; the current state, treatment, and next state with their numeric identifiers; `Reward`, the synthetic numeric reward used for learning; a data-quality score from 0 to 1; `IsTerminal`, which indicates whether the transition ends the episode; and `FinalOutcome`, which records the patient's clinical status after that decision (`Ongoing`, `Discharged`, or `Deceased`).
%[text] Specify the random number stream seed and algorithm for results reproducibility. For more information, see [Results Reproducibility](https://www.mathworks.com/help/reinforcement-learning/ug/train-reinforcement-learning-agents.html#mw_cfb4600e-9d19-4e4e-89c8-2749894fee3a).
rng(42);
%[text] Specify the synthetic EMR data file name and the number of unique patients in the table.
dataFilename = "sepsis_training_data.csv";
numPatients = 3000; 
%[text] If the file already exists, load it so repeated executions do not regenerate data. Otherwise, use the [`createSyntheticSepsisData`](internal:create_synthetic_data) local function. It takes the cache filename and number of patients as inputs and returns the synthetic EMR table and a label identifying its source.
if isfile(dataFilename)
    emr = readtable(dataFilename,TextType="string");
    dataSource = "Loaded cached synthetic CSV";
else
    [emr,dataSource] = createSyntheticSepsisData(dataFilename,numPatients);
end
%[text] Reset the random number generator after loading or generating the data. This way, the bootstrap samples, training, and simulations are identical whether the data was loaded from the cached file or regenerated.
rng(42);
%[text] Preview the first eight values of the six most important columns.
dataPreview = emr(1:min(8,height(emr)), ... %[output:group:3827708b] %[output:73382a69]
    ["PatientID" "ClinicalState" "Treatment" "Reward" ... %[output:73382a69]
    "DataQuality" "FinalOutcome"]) %[output:group:3827708b] %[output:73382a69]
%%
%[text] ## Define the State and Action Spaces and Summarize Data
%[text] A tabular approximation model needs a finite set of states and actions. The state represents the patient location in a simplified sepsis trajectory. The action represents one treatment decision available at that state.
%[text] Define explanatory states and action labels.
states = ["ED Well-Appearing"; "ED Febrile"; "ED Hypotensive"; ...
    "Sepsis Early"; "Sepsis Responding"; "Sepsis Worsening"; ...
    "Shock Fluid Responsive"; "Shock Refractory"; "MODS"; ...
    "ICU Stable"; "Floor Ready"; "Terminal"];
actions = ["Observe"; "Basic Labs"; "Sepsis Labs"; ...
    "Maintenance Fluids"; "1L Fluid Bolus"; "2L Fluid Bolus"; ...
    "Narrow Antibiotics"; "Broad Antibiotics"; ...
    "Low-Dose Vasopressor"; "High-Dose Vasopressor"; "Steroids"; ...
    "Intubation"; "Renal Replacement"; "Comfort Care"; "Discharge"];
actionLabels = ["Obs" "Lab" "Sep" "Mnt" "1L" "2L" "AbN" "AbB" "LoP" "HiP" "StR" "Int" "RRT" "Cmf" "D/C"];
%[text] Obtain the number of states and actions.
numStates = numel(states);
numActions = numel(actions);
%[text] Extract the state, action, reward, next-state, terminal, and data-quality histories from the table.
state = emr.StateCode;
action = emr.ActionCode;
reward = emr.Reward;
nextState = emr.NextStateCode;
isDone = logical(emr.IsTerminal);
dataQuality = emr.DataQuality;
%[text] Count the number of historical records for each state-action pair. 
stateActionCounts = accumarray([state action],1,[numStates numActions],@sum,0);
rowTotals = sum(stateActionCounts,2);
%[text] Normalize each row of counts to estimate the historical action probability conditional on state. The conservative adjustment later applies a larger penalty when this probability is smaller.
historicalActionProbability = stateActionCounts ./ max(rowTotals,1);
%[text] Use the [`patientLevelOutcome`](internal:patient_level_outcome) local function to collapse the decision-level EMR table into one outcome per patient. It takes the table as input and returns a string vector containing `Discharged`, `Deceased`, or `Ongoing` for each patient.
patientOutcome = patientLevelOutcome(emr);
%[text] Obtain the number of discharged, dead, and ongoing patients.
numDischarged = sum(patientOutcome == "Discharged");
numDeaths = sum(patientOutcome == "Deceased");
numOngoing = sum(patientOutcome == "Ongoing");
%[text] Summarize the data source characteristics in two tables.
dataSummary = table( ... %[output:group:179e73c6] %[output:594c5b99]
    ["Data source"; "Decision records"; "Unique patients"; ... %[output:594c5b99]
    "Historical mortality"; "State-action pairs with data"; ... %[output:594c5b99]
    "State-action pairs with at least 50 samples"], ... %[output:594c5b99]
    [dataSource; string(height(emr)); string(numel(patientOutcome)); ... %[output:594c5b99]
    string(100*numDeaths/numel(patientOutcome)) + "%"; ... %[output:594c5b99]
    string(nnz(stateActionCounts > 0)); ... %[output:594c5b99]
    string(nnz(stateActionCounts >= 50))], ... %[output:594c5b99]
    VariableNames=["Metric" "Value"]) %[output:group:179e73c6] %[output:594c5b99]
outcomeSummary = table(["Discharged"; "Deceased"; "Ongoing"], ... %[output:group:98b69f9c] %[output:7470c0eb]
    [numDischarged; numDeaths; numOngoing], ... %[output:7470c0eb]
    VariableNames=["PatientOutcome" "Count"]) %[output:group:98b69f9c] %[output:7470c0eb]
%%
%[text] ## Configure Tabular Approximation Model
%[text] Configure the model before learning. This section defines the learning rate, discount factor, mini-batch size, training epochs, bootstrap ensemble size, and training subset used by the Reinforcement Learning Toolbox trainer. The configuration is separate from learning so that the reader can see which values control the algorithm.
%[text] #### Set the learning hyperparameters 
%[text] These values are illustrative rather than clinically optimized. The critic is trained with the Adam optimizer; a learning rate of 0.1 is large by neural-network standards but reasonable here because each table cell is an independent parameter, and it keeps the runtime manageable. A discount factor of 0.99 retains the influence of delayed outcomes, and a mini-batch size of 64 balances update stability against cost.
learningRate = 0.1;
discountFactor = 0.99;
%[text] Use 50 training epochs as a compromise between runtime and refinement. In this example, each agent takes about 6 to 9 minutes to train, or about 36 minutes for all five agents on the computer used to produce these results, and the monitored Q-value estimate is still rising slowly after 50 epochs (see the training summary below), so more epochs would refine the values at a proportional runtime cost. Depending on the problem, this setting may need tuning.
numEpochs = 50;
miniBatchSize = 64;
%[text] Train five agents on different patient-level bootstrap samples (the bootstrap concept is explained [later in this example](internal:M_5dd4)). Their mean Q value is the working estimate, and their standard deviation is a simple estimate of sampling uncertainty.
numAgents = 5;
%[text] Select high-quality records and retain records with decisive outcomes, meaning very negative rewards (mostly deaths) and successful discharges, so the training archive represents both routine decisions and terminal events.
highQualityRows = find(dataQuality >= 0.85);
deathRows = find(reward < -50);
successfulDischargeRows = find(reward > 50 & action == 15);
%[text] Include high-quality decisions and records with decisive outcomes.
trainingRows = unique([highQualityRows; deathRows; successfulDischargeRows]);
numTrainingRows = numel(trainingRows);
%[text] Group the selected rows by patient. Sampling these groups preserves the within-patient sequence and avoids treating correlated decisions as independent bootstrap observations.
[~,~,trainingPatientIndex] = unique(emr.PatientID(trainingRows));
rowsByPatient = accumarray(trainingPatientIndex,trainingRows,[],@(rows){rows});
numTrainingPatients = numel(rowsByPatient);
modelSettings = table( ... %[output:group:4aa691f8] %[output:1225240b]
    ["Learning rate"; "Discount factor"; "Mini-batch size"; ... %[output:1225240b]
    "Training epochs"; "Bootstrap agents"; "Training rows"; ... %[output:1225240b]
    "High-quality rows"; "Death outcome rows"; ... %[output:1225240b]
    "Successful discharge rows"], ... %[output:1225240b]
    [learningRate; discountFactor; miniBatchSize; numEpochs; ... %[output:1225240b]
    numAgents; numTrainingRows; numel(highQualityRows); ... %[output:1225240b]
    numel(deathRows); ... %[output:1225240b]
    numel(successfulDischargeRows)], ... %[output:1225240b]
    VariableNames=["Setting" "Value"]) %[output:group:4aa691f8] %[output:1225240b]
%%
%[text] ## Learn from Historical Decisions
%[text] In this section, you train the five DQN agents, each with a tabular critic, on patient-level bootstrap samples of the same offline data. 
%[text] %[text:anchor:M_5dd4] ### Why Use the Bootstrap
%[text] The 3000 synthetic patients (`numPatients`) are one possible draw from everything that could have happened in a hospital. A different set of 3000 patients from the same hospital would produce a somewhat different Q table. Because you cannot collect those other cohorts, the bootstrap imitates them.
%[text] The 3000 patients produce 6721 decision records, about 2.2 decisions per patient on average. From these, 5566 records are selected for training (`numTrainingRows`). These records are grouped by patient into `rowsByPatient`, which gives `numTrainingPatients` training patients; patients with no selected records do not appear. For each of the 5 agents (`numAgents`), the example draws `numTrainingPatients` patients with replacement and keeps all of each sampled patient's training records together. Each resample has the same number of patients and, on average, the same case mix as the original. However, about a third of the patients (roughly 37%) are left out, and others appear two or more times in their place. Because patients have different numbers of records, the number of records in each resample, `numBootstrapRows`, varies around 5566. Each resample stands in for a plausible alternative patient cohort, and one agent is trained on each.
%[text] Each agent sees a different resampled patient mix. The mean across learned tables, `qMean`, supplies the action values used by the standard policy and the conservative adjustment. Their standard deviation, `qStd`, measures ensemble disagreement and is reported as uncertainty in the plots and recommendation audit; it does not affect action selection.
%[text] *Bootstrap disagreement* is useful because offline clinical data is uneven. If the agents agree, the estimate is less sensitive to which patients were sampled. If they disagree, the recommendation deserves extra review. Agreement does not reveal bias shared by every record, and state-action pairs with no records do not have a meaningful uncertainty estimate.
%[text] ### What the Bootstrap Cannot Tell
%[text] The *bootstrap measures sampling variability, not bias*. All five agents learn from resamples of the same archive, so if the archive is systematically biased, they all learn the same wrong lesson and agree with each other. Confounding by indication is the classic example. Sicker patients receive more aggressive treatment, and they die more often because they are sicker. From the data alone, aggressive treatment can therefore look harmful even if it is not. The agent compares treatments within the same state, so severity captured by the state definition is partly accounted for, but severity that the 12 states do not capture, such as differences in lactate or comorbidities within one state, can still mislead it. This limitation belongs to observational data rather than to reinforcement learning. It affects any method that learns from retrospective records without additional causal assumptions. The synthetic simulator in this example has no hidden severity, so confounding does not occur here, but it would be a central concern with real EMR data.
%[text] In the code below, `rlFiniteSetSpec` declares the finite states and actions, `rlTable` stores the Q values, and `rlQValueFunction` exposes the table to an `rlDQNAgent`. An `rlReplayMemory` holds the recorded transitions, and `trainFromData` trains each agent from this fixed archive without interacting with live patients.
%[text] During training, the example prints one line per agent. After training, a progress table reports each agent's runtime, final Q-value estimate, mean Q-value estimate over the last 10 epochs, and change across those epochs.
%[text] Define observation and action specifications.
obsInfo = rlFiniteSetSpec(1:numStates);
obsInfo.Name = "Clinical State";
actInfo = rlFiniteSetSpec(1:numActions);
actInfo.Name = "Treatment";
%[text] Define training parameters.
stepsPerEpoch = ceil(numTrainingRows/miniBatchSize);
qValueObservations = {reshape(1:numStates,1,1,numStates)};
qEnsemble = zeros(numStates,numActions,numAgents);
finalQEstimate = zeros(numAgents,1);
meanLast10QEstimate = zeros(numAgents,1);
last10QEstimateChange = zeros(numAgents,1);
agentElapsedSeconds = zeros(numAgents,1);
estimatedRemainingSeconds = zeros(numAgents,1);
%[text] Train each agent on one patient-level bootstrap sample.
trainingTimer = tic;
for agentIdx = 1:numAgents %[output:group:36fca55b]
    disp("Training bootstrap agent " + agentIdx + ... %[output:7b829d2c]
        " of " + numAgents + ".") %[output:7b829d2c]
    agentTimer = tic;
    % Resample whole patients with replacement so all decisions from a
    % sampled patient remain together.
    sampledPatients = randi(numTrainingPatients,numTrainingPatients,1);
    bootstrapRows = vertcat(rowsByPatient{sampledPatients});
    numBootstrapRows = numel(bootstrapRows);
    % Create table and critic objects.
    qTable = rlTable(obsInfo,actInfo);
    critic = rlQValueFunction(qTable,obsInfo,actInfo);
    % Configure the DQN agent for fixed-data training.
    agentOptions = rlDQNAgentOptions( ...
        DiscountFactor=discountFactor, ...
        MiniBatchSize=miniBatchSize, ...
        ExperienceBufferLength=numBootstrapRows, ...
        UseDoubleDQN=false, ...
        TargetSmoothFactor=1, ...
        TargetUpdateFrequency=stepsPerEpoch);
    agentOptions.CriticOptimizerOptions = rlOptimizerOptions( ...
        LearnRate=learningRate,GradientThreshold=1);
    agentOptions.EpsilonGreedyExploration.EpsilonMin = 0;
    agentOptions.EpsilonGreedyExploration.Epsilon = 0;
    agent = rlDQNAgent(critic,agentOptions);
    experienceBuffer = rlReplayMemory(obsInfo,actInfo,numBootstrapRows);
%[text] Use [`emrToExperiences`](internal:emr_to_experiences) to convert the selected table rows into the structure expected by the replay memory. It takes the transition arrays and row indices as inputs and returns a structure array of experiences.
    experiences = emrToExperiences( ...
        state,action,reward,nextState,isDone,bootstrapRows);
    append(experienceBuffer,experiences);
    agent.ExperienceBuffer = experienceBuffer;
    trainingOptions = rlTrainingFromDataOptions( ...
        MaxEpochs=numEpochs, ...
        NumStepsPerEpoch=stepsPerEpoch, ...
        QValueObservations=qValueObservations, ...
        Verbose=false, ...
        Plots="none");
    trainingStatistics = trainFromData(agent,trainingOptions);
%[text] Use [`readQTable`](internal:read_q_table) to extract the learned Q table from the trained critic. It takes the agent as input and returns the Q-value matrix, with one row per state and one column per action.
    qEnsemble(:,:,agentIdx) = readQTable(agent);
    finalQEstimate(agentIdx) = trainingStatistics.QValue(end);
    numRecentEpochs = min(10,numel(trainingStatistics.QValue));
    recentQEstimates = trainingStatistics.QValue( ...
        end-numRecentEpochs+1:end);
    meanLast10QEstimate(agentIdx) = mean(recentQEstimates);
    last10QEstimateChange(agentIdx) = ...
        recentQEstimates(end) - recentQEstimates(1);
    agentElapsedSeconds(agentIdx) = toc(agentTimer);
    averageAgentSeconds = mean(agentElapsedSeconds(1:agentIdx));
    estimatedRemainingSeconds(agentIdx) = ...
        averageAgentSeconds*(numAgents - agentIdx);
end %[output:group:36fca55b]
%[text] Display the training times and the average Q-values for the five agents.
progress = table((1:numAgents)',agentElapsedSeconds, ... %[output:group:26434856] %[output:2cce2bbd]
    estimatedRemainingSeconds,finalQEstimate,meanLast10QEstimate, ... %[output:2cce2bbd]
    last10QEstimateChange, ... %[output:2cce2bbd]
    VariableNames=["CompletedAgent" "AgentSeconds" ... %[output:2cce2bbd]
    "EstimatedRemainingSeconds" "FinalQValueEstimate" ... %[output:2cce2bbd]
    "MeanLast10QValue" "Last10QValueChange"]) %[output:group:26434856] %[output:2cce2bbd]
qMean = mean(qEnsemble,3);
qStd = std(qEnsemble,0,3);
[~,standardPolicy] = max(qMean,[],2);
trainingSummary = table((1:numAgents)',finalQEstimate, ... %[output:group:41ec511f] %[output:5b98310a]
    meanLast10QEstimate,last10QEstimateChange, ... %[output:5b98310a]
    squeeze(min(min(qEnsemble,[],1),[],2)), ... %[output:5b98310a]
    squeeze(max(max(qEnsemble,[],1),[],2)), ... %[output:5b98310a]
    VariableNames=["Agent" "FinalQValueEstimate" "MeanLast10QValue" ... %[output:5b98310a]
    "Last10QValueChange" "MinQ" "MaxQ"]) %[output:group:41ec511f] %[output:5b98310a]
%[text] `FinalQValueEstimate` is the average Q value that `trainFromData` reports across the specified clinical states after the final epoch. In this example, the five final estimates range from 74.42 to 78.17.
%[text] `MeanLast10QValue` smooths short-term variation in the reported estimate. `Last10QValueChange` is the final estimate minus the first estimate in that 10-epoch window. A value near zero suggests that the monitored estimate is leveling off. In this example, the changes range from 0.88 to 2.30, so the estimates are still rising slowly. This value does not by itself prove convergence or policy quality.
%[text] For each nonterminal state, retrieve how many historical records support the action that the standard policy selects. `sub2ind` converts each (state, action) pair into a single linear index into the count matrix, so one indexing operation retrieves all the counts.
rawPolicyEvidence = stateActionCounts(sub2ind(size(stateActionCounts), ...
    (1:numStates-1)',standardPolicy(1:end-1)));
rawPolicyTable = table(states(1:end-1),actions(standardPolicy(1:end-1)), ... %[output:group:98df088d] %[output:8a78c426]
    rawPolicyEvidence, ... %[output:8a78c426]
    VariableNames=["State" "RawRecommendedAction" "EvidenceCount"]) %[output:group:98df088d] %[output:8a78c426]
%%
%[text] ## Add a Conservative Safety Layer
%[text] Standard Q-learning can overvalue rare actions because one lucky historical outcome can create a large Q value. Apply a count-based conservative adjustment that penalizes actions with weak support in the historical behavior policy. Actions with no examples are blocked by assigning a very negative Q value. This is a transparent educational version of the conservative idea used in [Conservative Q-Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.04779).
%[text] Set the maximum penalty to 10 Q-value units.
conservativePenalty = 10;
%[text] Convert historical action probabilities into a penalty matrix. The most common action in a state receives the smallest penalty; actions rarely chosen in that state receive a penalty closer to 10. This heuristic is inspired by CQL algorithm but does not implement the published CQL objective.
supportPenalty = conservativePenalty*(1 - historicalActionProbability);
%[text] Subtract the support penalty from the ensemble-mean Q values.
qConservative = qMean - supportPenalty;
%[text] Treat no-data and very-rare actions as outside the safety envelope. Actions with no records receive a value of -200, below the lowest Q value learned in this example (about -89), so they are selected only when every action in a state is unseen, as in the `Terminal` state. Actions with one to four records receive an extra penalty of 50. This hard cutoff at five records targets only the rarest actions; the `WARNING` label and the unsupported-action metric use a broader threshold of 10 records.
neverSeen = stateActionCounts == 0;
veryRare = stateActionCounts > 0 & stateActionCounts < 5;
qConservative(neverSeen) = -200;
qConservative(veryRare) = qConservative(veryRare) - 50;
%[text] Select the action with the highest penalized Q value in each state. Element `conservativePolicy(s)` is the action index recommended for state `s`.
[~,conservativePolicy] = max(qConservative,[],2);
%[text] Use [`classifySafetyCounts`](internal:classify_safety_counts) to map the complete count matrix to numeric safety levels. It takes the count matrix as input and returns a same-sized matrix with levels from 0 (forbidden) to 3 (safe).
safetyLevel = classifySafetyCounts(stateActionCounts);
%[text] As for the standard policy, use `sub2ind` to retrieve the historical count supporting each conservative recommendation.
policyEvidence = stateActionCounts(sub2ind(size(stateActionCounts),(1:numStates)',conservativePolicy));
policySafety = strings(numStates,1);
%[text] Use [`safetyText`](internal:safety_text) to convert each recommendation count into a readable label. It takes one count as input and returns `FORBIDDEN`, `WARNING`, `CAUTION`, or `SAFE`.
for stateIdx = 1:numStates
    policySafety(stateIdx) = safetyText(policyEvidence(stateIdx));
end
%[text] Create and display a table that compares standard and conservative policies.
policyComparison = table(states,actions(standardPolicy), ... %[output:group:3a642c19] %[output:1185a9c2]
    actions(conservativePolicy),policyEvidence,policySafety, ... %[output:1185a9c2]
    VariableNames=["State" "StandardAction" "ConservativeAction" ... %[output:1185a9c2]
    "EvidenceCount" "SafetyLevel"]) %[output:group:3a642c19] %[output:1185a9c2]
%[text] In this example, the conservative adjustment changes one of the eleven nonterminal recommendations. In `Shock Refractory`, the standard policy recommends `Sepsis Labs`, an action with no historical records, while the conservative policy switches to `High-Dose Vasopressor`, which has 313 records. In the remaining states, both policies agree. In `MODS`, however, both policies choose `Steroids`, which rests on a single record and is labeled `WARNING`, even though `Intubation` has at least 50 records in that state. The learned value of `Steroids` is high enough to outweigh the extra penalty for very rare actions. The penalty discourages weak support but does not guarantee it, so inspect `SafetyLevel` rather than treating the policy as a hard safety constraint.
%%
%[text] ## Visualize Historical Coverage
%[text] The horizontal axis lists treatment actions and the vertical axis lists clinical states. Cell color is the base-10 logarithm of one plus the record count, not a learned value. Black circles mark the action selected by the conservative policy in each state.
imagesc(log10(stateActionCounts + 1)) %[output:98c47909]
colorbar %[output:98c47909]
title("Training Data Coverage") %[output:98c47909]
xlabel("Treatment Action") %[output:98c47909]
ylabel("Clinical State") %[output:98c47909]
xticks(1:numActions) %[output:98c47909]
xticklabels(actionLabels) %[output:98c47909]
xtickangle(45) %[output:98c47909]
yticks(1:numStates) %[output:98c47909]
yticklabels(states) %[output:98c47909]
hold on %[output:98c47909]
plot(conservativePolicy,1:numStates,"ko",MarkerSize=6,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:98c47909]
hold off %[output:98c47909]
%%
%[text] ## Visualize the Safety Envelope
%[text] This matrix uses the same action and state axes. Cell color converts historical counts into the four evidence categories, and black circles again show the conservative policy.
imagesc(safetyLevel) %[output:1737176d]
ax = gca;
colormap(ax,[0.80 0.10 0.10; 0.95 0.55 0.10; ... %[output:1737176d]
    0.95 0.85 0.25; 0.20 0.65 0.25]) %[output:1737176d]
cb = colorbar( ... %[output:1737176d]
    Ticks=[0 1 2 3], ... %[output:1737176d]
    TickLabels=["Forbidden" "Warning" "Caution" "Safe"]); %[output:1737176d]
cb.Label.String = "Safety Level"; %[output:1737176d]
title("Safety Envelope") %[output:1737176d]
xlabel("Treatment Action") %[output:1737176d]
ylabel("Clinical State") %[output:1737176d]
xticks(1:numActions) %[output:1737176d]
xticklabels(actionLabels) %[output:1737176d]
xtickangle(45) %[output:1737176d]
yticks(1:numStates) %[output:1737176d]
yticklabels(states) %[output:1737176d]
hold on %[output:1737176d]
plot(conservativePolicy,1:numStates,"ko",MarkerSize=6,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:1737176d]
hold off %[output:1737176d]
%%
%[text] ## Visualize Bootstrap Uncertainty
%[text] This matrix uses the same axes, but cell color now shows `qStd`, the standard deviation of learned Q values across bootstrap agents. Larger values indicate greater sensitivity to the sampled patient cohort. Cyan circles mark the conservative policy; gray cells have no historical records.
qStdForPlot = qStd;
qStdForPlot(neverSeen) = NaN;
imagesc(qStdForPlot,AlphaData=~isnan(qStdForPlot)) %[output:6297f410]
set(gca,Color=[0.85 0.85 0.85]) %[output:6297f410]
colorbar %[output:6297f410]
title("Bootstrap Uncertainty") %[output:6297f410]
xlabel("Treatment Action") %[output:6297f410]
ylabel("Clinical State") %[output:6297f410]
xticks(1:numActions) %[output:6297f410]
xticklabels(actionLabels) %[output:6297f410]
xtickangle(45) %[output:6297f410]
yticks(1:numStates) %[output:6297f410]
yticklabels(states) %[output:6297f410]
hold on %[output:6297f410]
plot(conservativePolicy,1:numStates,"co",MarkerSize=6,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:6297f410]
hold off %[output:6297f410]
%[text] Together, the three figures distinguish historical coverage, categorical evidence thresholds, and model disagreement. The thresholds are educational conventions: zero examples are forbidden, 1-9 are warnings, 10-49 require caution, and 50 or more are labeled safe.
%%
%[text] ## Validate Policies in Simulation
%[text] Compare four policies in the same synthetic simulator: 
%[text] 1. The *reference policy* selects, in each state, the treatment with the highest immediate value in the hand-authored simulator table.
%[text] 2. The *human-like policy* usually follows that reference but draws a random plausible action with probability 0.20.
%[text] 3. *Standard Q* selects the action with the highest ensemble-mean Q value in each state.
%[text] 4. *Conservative Q* does the same after applying the support penalty.  \
%[text] Define the number of validation patients and the maximum number of steps.
numValidationPatients = 1000;
maxSteps = 20;
%[text] Use [`defineReferenceClinicalValues`](internal:define_reference_values) to construct the simulator's hand-authored state-action value matrix. It takes the numbers of states and actions as inputs and returns a matrix with one value for every pair.
referenceQ = defineReferenceClinicalValues(numStates,numActions);
%[text] Get the index of the reference policy.
[~,referencePolicy] = max(referenceQ,[],2);
%[text] Use [`simulatePolicyTrial`](internal:simulate_policy_trial) to evaluate the deterministic reference policy. The function takes the policy type, action indices, trial size, horizon, simulator values, evidence counts, and human error rate as inputs; it returns survival, mortality, average episode length, and unsupported-action percentages. The final input is zero because fixed policies do not use an error rate.
[referenceSurvival,referenceMortality,referenceSteps,referenceUnsupported] = simulatePolicyTrial( ...
    "Fixed",referencePolicy,numValidationPatients,maxSteps, ...
    referenceQ,stateActionCounts,0);
%[text] Call [`simulatePolicyTrial`](internal:simulate_policy_trial) with policy type `Human` and error rate 0.20 to evaluate a human-like policy that draws a random plausible action on 20% of decisions. The random draw can occasionally coincide with the reference action.
[humanSurvival,humanMortality,humanSteps,humanUnsupported] = simulatePolicyTrial( ...
    "Human",referencePolicy,numValidationPatients,maxSteps, ...
    referenceQ,stateActionCounts,0.20);
%[text] Call [`simulatePolicyTrial`](internal:simulate_policy_trial) with the learned standard policy to evaluate its fixed action for each state. Pass 0 as the error rate; fixed policies ignore it.
[standardSurvival,standardMortality,standardSteps,standardUnsupported] = ...
    simulatePolicyTrial("Fixed",standardPolicy,numValidationPatients, ...
    maxSteps,referenceQ,stateActionCounts,0);
%[text] Call [`simulatePolicyTrial`](internal:simulate_policy_trial) with the conservative policy to measure the effect of the support penalty under the same simulator.
[conservativeSurvival,conservativeMortality,conservativeSteps,conservativeUnsupported] = simulatePolicyTrial( ...
    "Fixed",conservativePolicy,numValidationPatients,maxSteps, ...
    referenceQ,stateActionCounts,0);
%[text] Assemble and display a table showing validation results. 
policyNames = ["Reference"; "Human-Like"; "Standard Q"; "Conservative Q"];
survival = [referenceSurvival; humanSurvival; standardSurvival; conservativeSurvival];
mortality = [referenceMortality; humanMortality; standardMortality; conservativeMortality];
averageSteps = [referenceSteps; humanSteps; standardSteps; conservativeSteps];
unsupportedActions = [referenceUnsupported; humanUnsupported; standardUnsupported; conservativeUnsupported];
validationResults = table(policyNames,survival,mortality,averageSteps, ... %[output:group:3ccfce4c] %[output:9eb43ffb]
    unsupportedActions, ... %[output:9eb43ffb]
    VariableNames=["Policy" "SurvivalPercent" "MortalityPercent" ... %[output:9eb43ffb]
    "AverageSteps" "UnsupportedActionsPercent"]) %[output:group:3ccfce4c] %[output:9eb43ffb]
%[text] Compare Conservative Q with Human-Like using synthetic mortality. Absolute mortality risk reduction is the percentage-point difference in mortality. Relative mortality risk reduction divides that difference by Human-Like mortality. Number needed to treat is the reciprocal of the absolute reduction expressed as a proportion; it is reported only when simulated mortality decreases.
absoluteMortalityRiskReduction = humanMortality - conservativeMortality;
relativeMortalityRiskReduction = 100*absoluteMortalityRiskReduction/humanMortality;
if absoluteMortalityRiskReduction > 0
    numberNeededToTreat = 100/absoluteMortalityRiskReduction;
else
    numberNeededToTreat = NaN;
end
clinicalMetrics = table(absoluteMortalityRiskReduction, ... %[output:group:4f6522e2] %[output:7055840b]
    relativeMortalityRiskReduction,numberNeededToTreat) %[output:group:4f6522e2] %[output:7055840b]
%[text] The validation table highlights the tradeoff between simulated outcomes and historical support. `SurvivalPercent` and `MortalityPercent` summarize simulator outcomes, `AverageSteps` is the mean episode length, and `UnsupportedActionsPercent` is the fraction of decisions backed by fewer than 10 records. In this example, the reference policy reaches 94.9% simulated survival, the human-like policy 89.3%, Standard Q 91.2%, and Conservative Q 91.8%. The conservative adjustment lowers unsupported actions from 4.1% for Standard Q to 2.8%, about the same rate as the human-like policy. With 1000 simulated patients per policy, survival differences smaller than about 2.5 percentage points can arise from chance alone, so read Conservative Q and Standard Q as having similar outcomes. The same caution applies to the risk metrics: compared with the human-like policy, Conservative Q has an absolute mortality risk reduction of 2.5 percentage points, a relative reduction of 23.4%, and a number needed to treat of 40, but the absolute difference is at the edge of simulation noise. The reference policy remains about 3 percentage points ahead of both learned policies.
%[text] Both learned policies also need about 4.5 decisions per patient on average, compared with 2.1 for the reference policy. The reference policy picks the treatment with the highest immediate value, whereas the learned policies maximize the total reward over the whole episode. Because the synthetic reward gives a positive value to most treatment steps and the discount factor is 0.99, a longer route to discharge can collect more total reward than an immediate discharge. For example, both learned policies choose `Observe` instead of `Discharge` in `ED Well-Appearing` and `ICU Stable`. The longer routes also give simulated patients more opportunities to deteriorate, which likely explains part of the survival gap to the reference policy. This behavior is an effect of the reward design, not a clinical finding, and it shows why the reward definition needs as much clinical review as the learned policy.
%[text] **Because the simulator and data are synthetic, the risk metrics are demonstrations of calculation rather than estimates of a real clinical benefit.**
%%
%[text] ## Compare Outcomes and Unsupported Actions
%[text] Plot survival and unsupported-action rates together. A useful safety mechanism should reduce unsupported actions without hiding the effect on outcomes.
tiledlayout(1,2) %[output:077a20de]
nexttile %[output:077a20de]
bar(survival) %[output:077a20de]
title("Survival by Policy") %[output:077a20de]
ylabel("Survival (%)") %[output:077a20de]
ylim([0 100]) %[output:077a20de]
xticks(1:numel(policyNames)) %[output:077a20de]
xticklabels(policyNames) %[output:077a20de]
xtickangle(30) %[output:077a20de]
grid on %[output:077a20de]
nexttile %[output:077a20de]
bar(unsupportedActions) %[output:077a20de]
title("Unsupported Actions") %[output:077a20de]
ylabel("Actions with Fewer Than 10 Examples (%)") %[output:077a20de]
xticks(1:numel(policyNames)) %[output:077a20de]
xticklabels(policyNames) %[output:077a20de]
xtickangle(30) %[output:077a20de]
grid on %[output:077a20de]
%[text] Read these plots together: the support penalty is useful only if it reduces unsupported actions without an unacceptable change in simulated outcomes. The recommendation-level audit remains necessary because the penalty does not enforce a minimum count.
%%
%[text] ## Inspect an Expected Trajectory
%[text] Start many simulated patients in refractory shock state and follow the conservative policy. This rollout asks what states the simplified simulator predicts after the policy recommends treatment in a high-acuity state.
startState = find(states == "Shock Refractory");
numRollouts = 500;
%[text] Use [`rolloutStateProbability`](internal:rollout_state_probability) to estimate the state distribution over time. It takes the initial state, policy, rollout count, horizon, simulator values, and state count as inputs and returns a state-by-time matrix of percentages.
stateProbability = rolloutStateProbability(startState,conservativePolicy, ...
    numRollouts,maxSteps,referenceQ,numStates);
figure
imagesc(1:size(stateProbability,2),1:numStates,stateProbability) %[output:2574ef04]
colorbar %[output:2574ef04]
title("Expected Trajectory from Shock Refractory") %[output:2574ef04]
xlabel("Time Step") %[output:2574ef04]
ylabel("Clinical State") %[output:2574ef04]
yticks(1:numStates) %[output:2574ef04]
yticklabels(states) %[output:2574ef04]
%[text] The trajectory plot is a probabilistic forecast under the simplified simulator. Probability moving through responsive or stable states represents simulated recovery, while probability in the terminal state combines discharge and death. In this example, a little under half of the simulated patients reach the terminal state right after the first decision. From refractory shock, this transition represents death, and the simulator assigns at least 40% mortality to this state even for the best treatment. The remaining patients move to `Shock Fluid Responsive` and then through `Sepsis Responding`, `ICU Stable`, and `Floor Ready`, and almost all reach the terminal state by about step 6. These trajectories are properties of the synthetic simulator, not clinical forecasts.
%%
%[text] ## Audit One Recommendation
%[text] Inspect one state as a bedside-style audit. The table reports the selected action, the learned value, the uncertainty estimate, the evidence count, and the safety label.
%[text] Select the `Sepsis Early` state as the recommendation to audit. Look up its index by name instead of hard-coding a number.
auditState = find(states == "Sepsis Early");
%[text] Obtain the action recommended by the conservative policy.
auditAction = conservativePolicy(auditState);
%[text] Collect data from the recommended action.
recommendedAction = actions(auditAction);
recommendedQValue = qConservative(auditState,auditAction);
recommendationUncertainty = qStd(auditState,auditAction);
recommendationEvidence = stateActionCounts(auditState,auditAction);
%[text] Use [`safetyText`](internal:safety_text) to convert the recommendation's evidence count into a safety label.
recommendationSafety = safetyText(recommendationEvidence);
%[text] Use [`confidenceText`](internal:confidence_text) to convert the recommendation's ensemble standard deviation into a qualitative confidence label. It takes one uncertainty value as input and returns `HIGH`, `MEDIUM`, or `LOW`.
recommendationConfidence = confidenceText(recommendationUncertainty);
%[text] Build and display a table showing the audited data.
recommendationAudit = table(states(auditState),recommendedAction, ... %[output:group:2422c4ef] %[output:6f6fb232]
    recommendedQValue,recommendationUncertainty,recommendationEvidence, ... %[output:6f6fb232]
    recommendationSafety,recommendationConfidence, ... %[output:6f6fb232]
    VariableNames=["State" "RecommendedAction" "QValue" "Uncertainty" ... %[output:6f6fb232]
    "EvidenceCount" "SafetyLevel" "Confidence"]) %[output:group:2422c4ef] %[output:6f6fb232]
%[text] Use [`topActionsForState`](internal:top_actions_for_state) to rank alternatives immediately before displaying them. It takes the audited state, penalized Q values, uncertainty matrix, evidence counts, and action names as inputs and returns a table containing the five highest-valued actions.
auditAlternatives = topActionsForState(auditState,qConservative,qStd, ...
    stateActionCounts,actions);
auditAlternatives %[output:661ea502]
%[text] Compare the selected action with the alternatives using all three signals: penalized Q value, bootstrap uncertainty, and evidence count. In this example, `Broad Antibiotics` has a penalized Q value of 114.4, an uncertainty of 1.9, and 672 records, producing `SAFE` and `HIGH` labels. The next-ranked action, `1L Fluid Bolus`, has a Q value of 95.6 but only 22 records and nearly three times the uncertainty (5.4). The qualitative labels summarize support within the synthetic data; they do not establish clinical appropriateness.
%%
%[text] ## Example Takeaways
%[text] This example demonstrates an **offline reinforcement learning workflow using Reinforcement Learning Toolbox without live-patient exploration**. 
%[text] The **key idea is that historical coverage penalizes the learned values of weakly supported actions**.
%[text] The result has three intended takeaways:
%[text] - First, using the Reinforcement Learning Toolbox you can convert recorded `(state, action, reward, next state)` tuples into offline experience, which `trainFromData` can then use to **train a treatment policy**.
%[text] - Second, a **conservative count-based penalty** blocks unseen actions and discourages weakly supported actions, reducing - but not necessarily eliminating - unsupported recommendations.
%[text] - Third, **an audit view gives the user evidence, uncertainty, and alternatives** instead of only a terse treatment label. \
%[text] The limitations are as important as the results. The trained policy:
%[text] - *Cannot* discover treatments absent from the data
%[text] - *Cannot* account for bedside cues outside the state definition
%[text] - *Cannot* remove confounding factors from observational records.  \
%[text] Treat the workflow as an educational pattern for safety analysis, not as clinical validation.
%%
%[text] ## References
%[text] These references give context for the offline reinforcement learning method, the conservative safety adjustment, and the simplified sepsis disease model used in this educational demo.
%[text] - Sutton, R. S., and Barto, A. G. (2018). *Reinforcement Learning: An Introduction*, 2nd ed. MIT Press. This textbook introduces Q-learning and the Bellman update underlying the tabular agent trained by Reinforcement Learning Toolbox.
%[text] - Levine, S., Kumar, A., Tucker, G., and Fu, J. (2020). [Offline Reinforcement Learning: Tutorial, Review, and Perspectives on Open Problems](https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.01643). This review explains why offline RL learns from fixed historical data instead of exploring online.
%[text] - Kumar, A., Zhou, A., Tucker, G., and Levine, S. (2020). [Conservative Q-Learning for Offline Reinforcement Learning](https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.04779). This paper motivates the conservative penalty idea. The demo uses a transparent tabular count-based approximation, not the neural-network CQL objective from the paper.
%[text] - Komorowski, M., Celi, L. A., Badawi, O., Gordon, A. C., and Faisal, A. A. (2018). [The Artificial Intelligence Clinician learns optimal treatment strategies for sepsis in intensive care](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-018-0213-5). This clinical RL study motivates the sepsis decision-support scenario.
%[text] - Singer, M., Deutschman, C. S., Seymour, C. W., et al. (2016). [The Third International Consensus Definitions for Sepsis and Septic Shock (Sepsis-3)](https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2016.0287). This consensus paper provides clinical background for sepsis and septic shock.
%[text] - Osband, I., Blundell, C., Pritzel, A., and Van Roy, B. (2016). [Deep Exploration via Bootstrapped DQN](https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04621). This paper provides background for using bootstrap ensembles to estimate uncertainty. \
%[text] Copyright (c) 2026, The MathWorks, Inc.
%%
%[text] ## Local Functions
%[text] ### Create synthetic sepsis data
%[text] The [`createSyntheticSepsisData`](internal:create_synthetic_data) function creates synthetic decision records. Its inputs specify the CSV cache filename and number of patients; its outputs are the generated table and a source label.
function [data,dataSource] = createSyntheticSepsisData(filename,numPatients) %[text:anchor:create_synthetic_data]
%createSyntheticSepsisData - Create synthetic sepsis decision records
states = ["ED Well-Appearing"; "ED Febrile"; "ED Hypotensive"; ...
    "Sepsis Early"; "Sepsis Responding"; "Sepsis Worsening"; ...
    "Shock Fluid Responsive"; "Shock Refractory"; "MODS"; ...
    "ICU Stable"; "Floor Ready"; "Terminal"];
actions = ["Observe"; "Basic Labs"; "Sepsis Labs"; ...
    "Maintenance Fluids"; "1L Fluid Bolus"; "2L Fluid Bolus"; ...
    "Narrow Antibiotics"; "Broad Antibiotics"; ...
    "Low-Dose Vasopressor"; "High-Dose Vasopressor"; "Steroids"; ...
    "Intubation"; "Renal Replacement"; "Comfort Care"; "Discharge"];
numStates = numel(states);
numActions = numel(actions);
%[text] Use [`defineReferenceClinicalValues`](internal:define_reference_values) to create the state-action values that drive data generation.
referenceQ = defineReferenceClinicalValues(numStates,numActions);
[~,referencePolicy] = max(referenceQ,[],2);
maxRows = numPatients*20;
patientID = strings(maxRows,1);
clinicalState = strings(maxRows,1);
treatment = strings(maxRows,1);
nextClinicalState = strings(maxRows,1);
finalOutcome = strings(maxRows,1);
stateCode = zeros(maxRows,1);
actionCode = zeros(maxRows,1);
nextStateCode = zeros(maxRows,1);
outcome = zeros(maxRows,1);
dataQuality = zeros(maxRows,1);
isTerminal = false(maxRows,1);
row = 0;
initialStateProbability = ...
    [0.10 0.20 0.20 0.20 0.05 0.10 0.08 0.05 0.02 0 0 0];
sourceProbability = [0.40 0.20 0.15 0.10 0.05 0.05 0.05];
sourceQuality = [1.00 0.90 0.85 0.70 0.75 0.30 0.50];
sourceError = [0.05 0.10 0.15 0.25 0.20 0.50 0.40];
for patient = 1:numPatients
%[text] Use [`sampleDiscrete`](internal:sample_discrete) to draw the initial state and source indices from their probability vectors.
    currentState = sampleDiscrete(initialStateProbability);
    source = sampleDiscrete(sourceProbability);
    patientDone = false;
    step = 0;
    while ~patientDone && step < 20 && currentState ~= 12
        step = step + 1;
%[text] Use [`selectHistoricalAction`](internal:select_historical_action) to draw a source-dependent treatment, then use [`simulateClinicalStep`](internal:simulate_clinical_step) to generate the next transition.
        selectedAction = selectHistoricalAction(currentState,source, ...
            sourceError(source),referencePolicy,referenceQ);
        [newState,r,done,died] = simulateClinicalStep(currentState, ...
            selectedAction,referenceQ);
        row = row + 1;
        patientID(row) = "PT" + pad(string(patient),6,"left","0");
        clinicalState(row) = states(currentState);
        treatment(row) = actions(selectedAction);
        nextClinicalState(row) = states(newState);
        stateCode(row) = currentState;
        actionCode(row) = selectedAction;
        nextStateCode(row) = newState;
        outcome(row) = round(r,2);
        dataQuality(row) = sourceQuality(source);
        isTerminal(row) = done;
        if done && died
            finalOutcome(row) = "Deceased";
        elseif done
            finalOutcome(row) = "Discharged";
        else
            finalOutcome(row) = "Ongoing";
        end
        currentState = newState;
        patientDone = done;
    end
end
data = table(patientID(1:row),clinicalState(1:row), ...
    stateCode(1:row),treatment(1:row),actionCode(1:row), ...
    nextClinicalState(1:row),nextStateCode(1:row),outcome(1:row), ...
    dataQuality(1:row),isTerminal(1:row),finalOutcome(1:row), ...
    VariableNames=["PatientID" "ClinicalState" "StateCode" "Treatment" ...
    "ActionCode" "NextState" "NextStateCode" "Reward" "DataQuality" ...
    "IsTerminal" "FinalOutcome"]);
writetable(data,filename)
dataSource = "Generated synthetic CSV cache";
end
%%
%[text] ### Define reference clinical values
%[text] The [`defineReferenceClinicalValues`](internal:define_reference_values) function takes the state and action counts and returns the simulated state-action value matrix used by the synthetic data generator and simulator.
function referenceQ = defineReferenceClinicalValues(numStates,numActions) %[text:anchor:define_reference_values]
%defineReferenceClinicalValues - Define the simulator treatment values
referenceQ = -50*ones(numStates,numActions);
referenceQ(1,[1 2 15]) = [5 8 15];
referenceQ(2,[2 3 7 8]) = [12 18 10 8];
referenceQ(3,[3 5 6 8 9]) = [8 20 15 12 10];
referenceQ(4,[3 5 8]) = [10 15 25];
referenceQ(5,[1 4 7 15]) = [12 15 18 25];
referenceQ(6,[6 8 9 10]) = [12 15 20 15];
referenceQ(7,[5 6 8 9]) = [20 15 12 10];
referenceQ(8,[6 9 10 11]) = [5 10 18 12];
referenceQ(9,[10 11 12 13 14]) = [12 8 15 10 5];
referenceQ(10,[1 4 7 15]) = [15 12 10 30];
referenceQ(11,[1 15]) = [5 40];
referenceQ(12,:) = 0;
end
%%
%[text] ### Select a historical action
%[text] The [`selectHistoricalAction`](internal:select_historical_action) function takes a state, data-source index, source error rate, reference policy, and reference values. It returns a simulated treatment action.
function action = selectHistoricalAction(state,source,errorRate, ... %[text:anchor:select_historical_action]
    referencePolicy,referenceQ)
%selectHistoricalAction - Simulate a historical treatment choice
if rand > errorRate
    action = referencePolicy(state);
    return
end
validActions = find(referenceQ(state,:) > -45);
if isempty(validActions)
    validActions = 1:size(referenceQ,2);
end
if source == 2
    action = validActions(1);
elseif source == 4
    action = validActions(end);
elseif source == 7
    constrainedActions = [1 2 4 7];
    action = constrainedActions(randi(numel(constrainedActions)));
else
    action = validActions(randi(numel(validActions)));
end
end
%%
%[text] ### Sample a discrete distribution
%[text] The [`sampleDiscrete`](internal:sample_discrete) function takes a vector of nonnegative weights and returns one randomly sampled index.
function idx = sampleDiscrete(probability) %[text:anchor:sample_discrete]
%sampleDiscrete - Draw one index from a discrete probability vector
edges = cumsum(probability(:))/sum(probability);
idx = find(rand <= edges,1,"first");
end
%%
%[text] ### Summarize patient outcomes
%[text] The [`patientLevelOutcome`](internal:patient_level_outcome) function takes the decision-level table and returns one final-status label per patient.
function outcome = patientLevelOutcome(data) %[text:anchor:patient_level_outcome]
%patientLevelOutcome - Collapse record outcomes to one outcome per patient
patient = unique(data.PatientID);
outcome = strings(numel(patient),1);
for idx = 1:numel(patient)
    rows = data.PatientID == patient(idx);
    patientOutcomes = string(data.FinalOutcome(rows));
    if any(patientOutcomes == "Deceased")
        outcome(idx) = "Deceased";
    elseif any(patientOutcomes == "Discharged")
        outcome(idx) = "Discharged";
    else
        outcome(idx) = "Ongoing";
    end
end
end
%%
%[text] ### Classify evidence counts
%[text] The [`classifySafetyCounts`](internal:classify_safety_counts) function takes a matrix of evidence counts and returns a same-sized matrix of numeric safety levels.
function safetyLevel = classifySafetyCounts(counts) %[text:anchor:classify_safety_counts]
%classifySafetyCounts - Convert evidence counts to safety levels
safetyLevel = zeros(size(counts));
safetyLevel(counts >= 1 & counts < 10) = 1;
safetyLevel(counts >= 10 & counts < 50) = 2;
safetyLevel(counts >= 50) = 3;
end
%%
%[text] ### Label evidence counts
%[text] The [`safetyText`](internal:safety_text) function takes one evidence count and returns its categorical safety label.
function label = safetyText(count) %[text:anchor:safety_text]
%safetyText - Convert an evidence count to a safety label
if count >= 50
    label = "SAFE";
elseif count >= 10
    label = "CAUTION";
elseif count >= 1
    label = "WARNING";
else
    label = "FORBIDDEN";
end
end
%%
%[text] ### Label ensemble uncertainty
%[text] The [`confidenceText`](internal:confidence_text) function takes one ensemble standard deviation and returns a qualitative confidence label.
function label = confidenceText(uncertainty) %[text:anchor:confidence_text]
%confidenceText - Convert ensemble uncertainty to a confidence label
if uncertainty < 5
    label = "HIGH";
elseif uncertainty < 15
    label = "MEDIUM";
else
    label = "LOW";
end
end
%%
%[text] ### Rank alternative actions
%[text] The [`topActionsForState`](internal:top_actions_for_state) function takes a state index, Q values, uncertainty values, evidence counts, and action names. It returns a table of the five highest-valued actions.
function alternatives = topActionsForState(state,qValues,uncertainty,counts,actions) %[text:anchor:top_actions_for_state]
%topActionsForState - Rank audited actions for one clinical state
[sortedQ,order] = sort(qValues(state,:),"descend");
topCount = min(5,numel(order));
selected = order(1:topCount);
evidence = counts(state,selected)';
safety = strings(topCount,1);
confidence = strings(topCount,1);
for idx = 1:topCount
%[text] Use [`safetyText`](internal:safety_text) and [`confidenceText`](internal:confidence_text) to label each ranked alternative.
    safety(idx) = safetyText(evidence(idx));
    confidence(idx) = confidenceText(uncertainty(state,selected(idx)));
end
alternatives = table(actions(selected),sortedQ(1:topCount)', ...
    uncertainty(state,selected)',evidence,safety,confidence, ...
    VariableNames=["Action" "QValue" "Uncertainty" ...
    "EvidenceCount" "SafetyLevel" "Confidence"]);
end
%%
%[text] ### Simulate policy outcomes
%[text] The [`simulatePolicyTrial`](internal:simulate_policy_trial) function takes a policy specification and simulator settings. It returns survival, mortality, average episode length, and unsupported-action percentages.
function [survivalPercent,mortalityPercent,averageSteps, ...
    unsupportedPercent] = simulatePolicyTrial(policyKind,policy, ... %[text:anchor:simulate_policy_trial]
    numPatients,maxSteps,referenceQ,stateActionCounts,errorRate)
%simulatePolicyTrial - Estimate policy outcomes in the synthetic simulator
survivors = 0;
deaths = 0;
totalSteps = 0;
unsupportedActions = 0;
totalActions = 0;
initialStateProbability = ...
    [0.10 0.20 0.20 0.20 0.05 0.10 0.08 0.05 0.02 0 0 0];
for patient = 1:numPatients
%[text] Use [`sampleDiscrete`](internal:sample_discrete) to draw an initial state.
    currentState = sampleDiscrete(initialStateProbability);
    done = false;
    reward = 0;
    step = 0;
    while ~done && step < maxSteps && currentState ~= 12
        step = step + 1;
        if policyKind == "Human"
%[text] Use [`selectHumanAction`](internal:select_human_action) to generate a noisy reference-policy action.
            selectedAction = selectHumanAction(currentState,referenceQ, ...
                errorRate);
        else
            selectedAction = policy(currentState);
        end
        totalActions = totalActions + 1;
        if stateActionCounts(currentState,selectedAction) < 10
            unsupportedActions = unsupportedActions + 1;
        end
%[text] Use [`simulateClinicalStep`](internal:simulate_clinical_step) to advance the simulated patient by one decision.
        [currentState,reward,done] = simulateClinicalStep(currentState, ...
            selectedAction,referenceQ);
    end
    if currentState == 12 && reward > 0
        survivors = survivors + 1;
    elseif currentState == 10 || currentState == 11
        survivors = survivors + 1;
    else
        deaths = deaths + 1;
    end
    totalSteps = totalSteps + step;
end
survivalPercent = 100*survivors/numPatients;
mortalityPercent = 100*deaths/numPatients;
averageSteps = totalSteps/numPatients;
unsupportedPercent = 100*unsupportedActions/max(totalActions,1);
end
%%
%[text] ### Select a human-like action
%[text] The [`selectHumanAction`](internal:select_human_action) function takes a state, reference-value matrix, and error rate. It returns either the reference action or a randomly selected plausible action.
function action = selectHumanAction(state,referenceQ,errorRate) %[text:anchor:select_human_action]
%selectHumanAction - Simulate a noisy human-like policy action
[~,referencePolicy] = max(referenceQ,[],2);
if rand > errorRate
    action = referencePolicy(state);
else
    validActions = find(referenceQ(state,:) > -45);
    if isempty(validActions)
        validActions = 1:size(referenceQ,2);
    end
    action = validActions(randi(numel(validActions)));
end
end
%%
%[text] ### Roll out state probabilities
%[text] The [`rolloutStateProbability`](internal:rollout_state_probability) function takes an initial state, policy, rollout count, horizon, simulator values, and state count. It returns the percentage of rollouts in each state at each time.
function stateProbability = rolloutStateProbability(startState,policy, ...
    numRollouts,maxSteps,referenceQ,numStates) %[text:anchor:rollout_state_probability]
%rolloutStateProbability - Estimate state probabilities over rollout time
stateCounts = zeros(numStates,maxSteps + 1);
for rollout = 1:numRollouts
    currentState = startState;
    stateCounts(currentState,1) = stateCounts(currentState,1) + 1;
    for step = 1:maxSteps
        if currentState == 12
            stateCounts(currentState,step+1) = stateCounts(currentState,step+1) + 1;
        else
            selectedAction = policy(currentState);
%[text] Use [`simulateClinicalStep`](internal:simulate_clinical_step) to advance each rollout.
            [currentState,~,done] = simulateClinicalStep(currentState, ...
                selectedAction,referenceQ);
            stateCounts(currentState,step+1) = stateCounts(currentState,step+1) + 1;
            if done && step < maxSteps
                stateCounts(currentState,step+2:end) = ...
                    stateCounts(currentState,step+2:end) + 1;
                break
            end
        end
    end
end
stateProbability = 100*stateCounts/numRollouts;
end
%%
%[text] ### Simulate one clinical step
%[text] The [`simulateClinicalStep`](internal:simulate_clinical_step) function takes a state, action, and reference-value matrix. It returns the next state, noisy reward, terminal flag, and death flag.
function [nextState,reward,isDone,died] = simulateClinicalStep(state,action,referenceQ) %[text:anchor:simulate_clinical_step]
%simulateClinicalStep - Advance one synthetic sepsis treatment step
isDone = false;
died = false;
reward = referenceQ(state,action) + 5*randn;
quality = referenceQ(state,action);
switch state
    case 1
        if action == 15
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            reward = reward + 30;
        elseif quality > 5
            nextState = 5;
        else
            nextState = 2;
        end
    case 2
        if quality > 15
            nextState = 5;
        elseif quality > 8
            nextState = 4;
        else
            nextState = 3;
        end
    case 3
        if quality > 15
            nextState = 7;
        elseif quality > 10
            nextState = 4;
        elseif quality > 0
            nextState = 8;
        elseif rand < 0.15
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            died = true;
            reward = -100;
        else
            nextState = 8;
        end
    case 4
        if quality > 20
            nextState = 5;
        elseif quality > 10
            nextState = 6;
        else
            nextState = 8;
        end
    case 5
        if action == 15
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            reward = reward + 30;
        elseif quality > 5
            nextState = 10;
        else
            nextState = 6;
        end
    case 6
        if quality > 15
            nextState = 5;
        elseif quality > 10
            nextState = 7;
        elseif quality > 0 && rand >= 0.15
            nextState = 8;
        elseif quality <= 0 && rand >= 0.30
            nextState = 8;
        else
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            died = true;
            reward = -100;
        end
    case 7
        if quality > 15
            nextState = 5;
        elseif quality > 10 && rand >= 0.10
            nextState = 10;
        elseif quality > 0 && rand >= 0.25
            nextState = 8;
        elseif quality <= 0 && rand >= 0.35
            nextState = 8;
        else
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            died = true;
            reward = -100;
        end
    case 8
        mortality = 0.40;
        if quality < 5
            mortality = 0.70;
        elseif quality < 10
            mortality = 0.55;
        end
        if rand < mortality
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            died = true;
            reward = -100;
        elseif quality > 15
            nextState = 7;
        else
            nextState = 9;
        end
    case 9
        mortality = 0.60;
        if quality < 8
            mortality = 0.80;
        elseif quality < 12
            mortality = 0.70;
        end
        if rand < mortality
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            died = true;
            reward = -100;
        elseif quality > 12
            nextState = 8;
        else
            nextState = 9;
        end
    case 10
        if action == 15
            nextState = 12;
            isDone = true;
            reward = reward + 25;
        elseif quality > 10 || rand >= 0.05
            nextState = 11;
        else
            nextState = 6;
        end
    case 11
        nextState = 12;
        isDone = true;
        if action == 15
            reward = reward + 30;
        else
            reward = reward + 15;
        end
    otherwise
        nextState = 12;
        isDone = true;
end
if rand < 0.08 && any(state == [1 2 3 4 5 6 7]) && ~isDone
    if state < 6
        nextState = min(state + 2,8);
    else
        nextState = min(nextState + 1,9);
    end
    reward = reward - 15;
end
if any(state == [6 7 8 9]) && ~isDone && rand < 0.05
    nextState = 12;
    isDone = true;
    died = true;
    reward = -100;
end
end
%%
%[text] ### Convert EMR rows to experiences
%[text] The [`emrToExperiences`](internal:emr_to_experiences) function takes transition arrays and selected row indices. It returns a structure array compatible with `rlReplayMemory`.
function experiences = emrToExperiences(state,action,reward,nextState,isDone,rows) %[text:anchor:emr_to_experiences]
%emrToExperiences - Convert recorded EMR rows to RL Toolbox experiences
experiences = struct( ...
    "Observation",arrayfun(@(value){double(value)}, ...
    state(rows),UniformOutput=false), ...
    "Action",arrayfun(@(value){double(value)}, ...
    action(rows),UniformOutput=false), ...
    "Reward",num2cell(double(reward(rows))), ...
    "NextObservation",arrayfun(@(value){double(value)}, ...
    nextState(rows),UniformOutput=false), ...
    "IsDone",num2cell(isDone(rows)));
end
%%
%[text] ### Read the learned Q table
%[text] The [`readQTable`](internal:read_q_table) function takes a trained agent and returns its learned Q-value matrix. `getModel` returns the `rlTable` object stored in the critic, and the `Table` property of that object holds the Q values, with one row per state and one column per action.
function qValues = readQTable(agent) %[text:anchor:read_q_table]
%readQTable - Return the learned Q table of a tabular RL Toolbox agent
tableModel = getModel(getCritic(agent));
qValues = tableModel.Table;
end

